In modern MSTest (v3+), `TestContext` is injected via the constructor, and you can use `[TestInitialize]` (which supports `async Task`) for async setup:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private MyService _service;

    // Constructor injection for TestContext (MSTest v3+)
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;

        // Synchronous initialization here
        _service = new MyService();
    }

    // Async initialization
    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task InitAsync()
    {
        await _service.ConnectAsync();
        _testContext.WriteLine("Initialized");
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task MyTest()
    {
        var result = await _service.GetDataAsync();
        Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- **Constructor** — use for sync setup and receiving `TestContext` via DI (MSTest v3+).
- **`[TestInitialize]`** — supports `async Task` for async setup; runs before each test.
- **`[ClassInitialize]`** — for one-time async setup shared across all tests in the class (must be `static`).

> **Note:** Constructor injection of `TestContext` replaced the legacy `public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }` property pattern from MSTest v1/v2.
